## Bug

A checkbox that is both `checked` and `disabled` renders its checkmark in near-zero contrast against its own background, making it effectively invisible. Visually, a disabled-but-checked checkbox looks unchecked/empty even though it is checked.

Screenshot:

<img width="364" height="133" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/18f3d3d1-1fc3-4e9b-b92a-7ccd3cb80773" />

Compare with stock checkbox:

<img width="31" height="31" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/9ff2ae8a-f874-4c6b-9a18-1c70d8229096" />

## Cause

In `src/assets/properties-panel.css`:

```css
.bio-properties-panel-input[type="checkbox"]:checked {
  border-color: var(--checkbox-checked-border-color);
  background-color: var(--checkbox-checked-background-color);
  background-image: url("data:image/svg+xml,...stroke='%23fff'...");
}

.bio-properties-panel-input[type="checkbox"]:disabled {
  cursor: not-allowed;
  border-color: var(--input-disabled-border-color);
  background-color: var(--input-disabled-background-color);
}
```
(`src/assets/properties-panel.css:696-706`)

- The `:checked` rule (line 696) sets `background-color` to the accent color and paints a white (`%23fff`) checkmark via `background-image` (line 699).
- The `:disabled` rule (line 702) has equal specificity and comes later in the stylesheet, so when both apply it overrides `background-color` to a near-white/light-grey color (`--input-disabled-background-color`, which resolves to `--color-grey-225-10-97`).
- Crucially, `:disabled` does **not** override `background-image`. The white checkmark SVG from the `:checked` rule remains applied, now painted on a near-white/light-grey background instead of the accent color — resulting in a checkmark with almost no contrast against its background.

## Why it matters

Any Boolean property that is read-only and `true` by default (`editable: false`, `value: true`) renders via this shared component/CSS, in both Desktop Modeler and Web Modeler. Because the checkmark is effectively invisible, a checked-but-disabled checkbox is visually indistinguishable from an unchecked one, misleading users about the property's actual value.

This was surfaced while validating a properties panel entry in `camunda/connectors` PR #8550, which added the first read-only checked Boolean property (OpenAI/Gemini "Prompt caching") to actually exercise this checked+disabled combination — the underlying CSS characteristic predates that PR and isn't specific to it.

## Reproduction

Render any `<input type="checkbox">` with both the `checked` and `disabled` attributes present, using this stylesheet. The checkbox will appear empty/unchecked despite being checked.

## Suggested fix

Give the disabled+checked state its own rule (or extend `:disabled` when combined with `:checked`) that keeps sufficient contrast between the checkmark and its background — e.g. either preserve the accent-colored (or a suitably dark) background for `:checked:disabled`, or override `background-image` to use a checkmark color with adequate contrast against `--input-disabled-background-color`.

The contrast of the boxes is weak. Hardly visible.
Of course, this could be changed with CSS: 
```
.form-check-input {
border: 1px solid #fff;
}
```
In WCAG 2.1, the border contrasts are not defined, but for WCAG 2.2 this will have to be stronger.

